# src/domain/market-events-data.ts: 13+ concurrently open research PRs each append one entry to
# this one shared array (#1324, measured 2026-09-04) — merging any single one shifts surrounding
# lines enough that every other open PR conflicts against the new base, even though their content
# never actually disagrees. A plain `merge=union` was tested and rejected: it merges by LINE, not
# by object, so two multi-line entries inserted at the same spot get interleaved into one malformed,
# duplicate-keyed object — silent data loss. scripts/merge-market-events.mjs merges at the object
# level instead. The line below only names the driver; its COMMAND lives in .git/config, which git
# cannot read from a tracked file — scripts/register-merge-drivers.sh writes it, called from the
# SessionStart hook and from worktree-setup.sh so every lane has it. GitHub's own "mergeable" check
# can't run a custom driver, so this only helps a local `git merge` — which is how these PRs
# actually get resolved.
src/domain/market-events-data.ts merge=market-events

# docs/research/forward-tests.md: the same append collision on the sibling ledger (#1334, measured
# 2026-09-05) — 13 of 24 conflict-flagged PRs since 09-03 named this file, and all 25 ledger commits
# on main since 09-04 are pure tail appends, so any two open research PRs conflict by construction.
# Here `merge=union` IS the right tool, for the reason it was wrong above: a registration is exactly
# ONE LINE (one table row), which is the unit union merges, so two appends both survive intact and
# in order. It is a git BUILT-IN, so unlike merge=market-events it needs no .git/config command and
# reaches every clone with no registration step. Do NOT widen this to docs/research/events/*.md or
# *.md — those carry multi-line prose, where union interleaves (scikit-learn hit exactly that and
# reverted). Union's blind spot is two sides editing the SAME row differently: it keeps both as two
# rows rather than conflicting. scripts/forward-test-id-scan.mjs reports the duplicate id, and that
# is the same outcome today's hand-merge produces. GitHub's server merge ignores .gitattributes, so
# this helps the local `git merge` the repair lane and ship.sh both run — which is how these PRs
# actually get resolved.
docs/research/forward-tests.md merge=union
